  ],
  "text": "Andromeda is heading straight at us at about a hundred and ten kilometres a second. In roughly four billion years, the two galaxies merge. Which sounds like the worst news anyone has ever delivered. But almost nothing will actually collide. Not one star is likely to hit another. Because a galaxy is mostly empty. Overwhelmingly, unimaginably empty. If the Sun were a grain of sand, the nearest star would be kilometres away. Scale two clouds like that up, pass them through each other, and they simply interpenetrate. What does change is the shape. Gravity drags both into long streams, then one blob. The Sun will probably get flung into a different orbit, and carry on completely unharmed. Two galaxies will pass right through each other and barely scratch a thing. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
